---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { tree, percent, scenarioWindow } from '../lib/tree';

const headline = tree.tiers.find((t) => t.key === tree.headline_tier);
const numbers = tree.numbers;
const equationFactors = tree.factors.filter((f) => f.letter !== 'C');
const contact = tree.factors.find((f) => f.letter === 'C');
---

<Base>
  <section class="hero wide">
    <p class="eyebrow">A public, long-horizon prediction project</p>
    <h1 class="question">What is the probability that I become a Belter before I die?</h1>
    <p class="lede">And how does that number move year over year?</p>

    <p class="equation" aria-label="The equation">
      <span class="term">P(Belter)</span><span class="op">=</span>
      {equationFactors.map((f, i) => (
        <>
          <a class="term" href={f.path} title={f.name}>{f.letter}</a>
          {i < equationFactors.length - 1 && <span class="op">×</span>}
        </>
      ))}
      {contact && <a class="beside" href={contact.path}>with C beside it, never multiplied in</a>}
    </p>
    <ul class="terms">
      {equationFactors.map((f) => (
        <li><span class="letter">{f.letter}</span><span><a href={f.path}><strong>{f.name}.</strong></a> {f.meaning}</span></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section narrow prose">
    <p>
      The Belt Equation takes a question most people file under science fiction, whether a settled solar
      system like the one in <em>The Expanse</em> arrives within a single human lifetime, and breaks it into a
      handful of factors anyone can argue about, the way the Drake Equation did for the number of
      civilizations in the galaxy. Under each factor sits a tree of concrete breakthroughs, each with its own
      probability, its own test for whether it has happened, and its own revision history. The headline number
      is computed from the tree, never chosen by hand, and the whole thing is re-estimated on a fixed schedule
      and published so other people can disagree with specific pieces of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The "I" in the question is a real person with a real expected lifespan, which is what turns an abstract
      forecast into something you can track your own life against. Anyone can run their own version.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shead">
      <h2>The headline number</h2>
      <span>{headline ? `${headline.key}, ${headline.name.toLowerCase()}` : ''}, under each of the five longevity scenarios</span>
    </div>
    <div class="scenarios">
      {tree.scenarios.map((s) => (
        <div class="scenario">
          <span class="sname">{s.key}</span>
          <span class="swindow">{s.name}</span>
          <span class="swindow dim">window: {scenarioWindow(s)}</span>
          {numbers.available && headline
            ? <span class="svalue">{percent(numbers.tiers[headline.key][s.key])}</span>
            : <span class="svalue pending">not yet</span>}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="dim" style="font-size: 14px; margin-top: 12px;">
      {numbers.available
        ? `Computed ${numbers.computed_on} from ${numbers.runs.toLocaleString()} play-throughs of the tree per scenario.${numbers.review_status ? ' ' + numbers.review_status : ''}`
        : `The numbers wait on the first probabilities (roadmap step 9): ${numbers.reason}`}
      {' '}<a href="/method/">How the numbers are computed.</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shead">
      <h2>The tree</h2>
      <span>{tree.counts.nodes} breakthroughs across eight factors, plus the Contact Clause</span>
    </div>
    <div class="fgrid">
      {tree.factors.map((f) => (
        <a class={`fcard ${f.letter === 'C' ? 'contact' : ''}`} href={f.path}>
          <span class="fletter">{f.letter}</span>
          <h3>{f.name}</h3>
          <p>{f.meaning}</p>
          <p class="fcount">{f.node_count} nodes · {f.with_probability} with probabilities{f.resolved ? ` · ${f.resolved} resolved` : ''}</p>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="dim" style="font-size: 14px; margin-top: 14px;">
      A picture of the whole tree, with its dependencies drawn and colored by factor, arrives at website step 22. Until then this grid is the map.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
